\name{Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor}
\alias{Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor}
\title{ggplot2 Error Bar Layer Processor}
\description{
Processes ggplot2's uncertainty geoms -- \code{geom_errorbar()},
\code{geom_errorbarh()}, \code{geom_linerange()}, \code{geom_pointrange()} and
\code{geom_crossbar()} -- into MAIDR's \code{error_bar} layer.

Uncertainty is usually the finding rather than the decoration: whether two
group means differ is answered by whether their intervals overlap. Until
this processor existed every one of these geoms fell through to
\code{Ggplot2UnknownLayerProcessor}, so the interval was dropped and that
comparison was unavailable to a MAIDR reader.
\subsection{Reading the right pair of bounds}{

The trap this class exists to avoid is that ggplot2's built data carries
\strong{both} pairs for most of these geoms, and only one of them is the
interval. A vertical \code{geom_errorbar()} computes:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{  x  y  ymin ymax  xmin  xmax  flipped_aes
  1 4.2  3.8  4.6  0.55  1.45  FALSE
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{ymin}/\code{ymax} are the interval; \code{xmin}/\code{xmax} are the \emph{cap width} -- how
wide the little crossbars are drawn, which is a styling parameter and not
data at all. Reading the wrong pair yields a chart describing the cap
geometry, which is both wrong and plausible-looking.

Which pair is the interval is decided by the layer's orientation, and
ggplot2 records that in two different ways depending on the geom:

\itemize{
\item \code{geom_errorbar(orientation = "y")} and friends set a \code{flipped_aes}
column to \code{TRUE} in the built data.
\item \code{geom_errorbarh()} has no \code{flipped_aes} column at all -- it is
horizontal by construction.
}

Both are handled, because a layer that read only \code{flipped_aes} would treat
every \code{geom_errorbarh()} as vertical and emit the cap heights as the
interval.
}
}
